 "web": "These barriers land on the same people at once. One household: the mother works a night shift assembling medical devices for a wage that covers about 2.7 people at the poverty line (Barrier 1). Her brother sells at the Sunday market without a pension (2). The house on the canyon slope has no deed yet (3), and the water truck comes on Tuesdays (4). Her husband was deported from California after twenty years, so his U.S.-born daughter visits him on weekends (5, 8). When the youngest got sick, the clinic was whichever one their work status allowed (7). The husband's Mexican voter ID expired years ago in California, so he can't sign up for the city's 3% small-business loan (10). When the family needs cement for the second floor, it borrows from a store-credit plan or a neighbor's *tanda* instead (11). And when a film crew from Denver arrives, the only version of the family that might reach an audience is the one somebody else edits (9). No single barrier decided this family's life. Together they set the price. *(A composite for teaching, not a person. Do not present it as a family students met.)*",
