 "cushionNotice": "Stack the rows twice for Tijuana, once for a worker at a large plant and once for a vendor, and the gap between them is nearly as wide as the gap between Tijuana and Denver. The plant worker's cushions are real, built by law and paid for by contributions she sees on her pay stub. Most RJ families have five or six cushions they did not build and rarely see. Read the papers and borrowing rows together: the same documents that get a Tijuana worker into IMSS also get her the 3% loan instead of the 58% one. **And one cushion appears only here: a passport.** It lets a Denver family cross for work, care or family in twenty minutes. That is what precarity means, and it is the fence we can't see from inside (2b).",
 "fences": [
  {
   "h": "Insulation",
   "text": "Paid leave, health insurance, a deed, a mortgage at 7% instead of a microloan at 58–73% (Barrier 11), a full set of papers nobody questions (Barrier 10), and a U.S. passport. Because those cushions are invisible from inside, someone else's fall looks like a choice. See 2c."
  },
  {
   "h": "Physical",
   "text": "We cross in minutes; many people we meet cannot cross at all. We see Tijuana from a van, a host family's table and a shelter's serving line, and never from a night shift. We are always passing through a place that, for other people, is a destination or a wall."
  },
  {
   "h": "Cognitive",
   "text": "We arrive already knowing Tijuana: cartels, cheap pharmacies, Avenida Revolución, the border crisis from cable news. We think of deportees as strangers to Mexico, when many grew up in Los Angeles or Denver. We think maquiladora means sweatshop or means opportunity; it's a specific job with a specific wage (Barrier 1). We don't know the Bracero Program, NAFTA or 1996 immigration law, so what we see looks like how it has always been."
  },
  {
   "h": "Affective",
   "text": "Fear: of the city, of the people at the shelter, of saying the wrong thing in Spanish. Pity: needing people to be sad so the film means something. Guilt that turns into a hurry to fix something. And the opposite fence: they're so happy despite everything, which erases the structure."
  },
  {
   "h": "Theological",
   "text": "We're so blessed, which reads a passport as God's favor and a deportation order as its absence. Treating the trip as bringing God to Tijuana, when the shelters, parishes and Dreamers' Moms were praying and working there long before we came."
  },
  {
   "h": "The camera",
   "text": ""
  }
 ],
